        <h2 id="about-heading">What is the Pomodoro Technique?</h2>
        <p><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomodoro_Technique" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pomodoro Technique</a> breaks work into short intervals, usually 25 minutes of focus (called Pomodoro) followed by a 5-minute break. After four Pomodoros you take a longer break before starting again.</p>
        <h3>Why it works:</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Easier to start.</strong>
            Twenty-five minutes feels small enough that starting doesn't feel like a big decision. Once you begin, most of the resistance disappears within a few minutes.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Breaks that actually happen.</strong>
            Without a timer, breaks get skipped or they run too long. A scheduled break means you rest before you're exhausted, not after.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Backed by how attention works.</strong>
            Staying focused takes mental energy, the way physical work tires a muscle. Working past that point leads to more mistakes. Short breaks restore that energy.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>It rewards discipline.</strong>
            The timer only sets the schedule — the real benefit comes from what you do inside it: full focus, then a real break.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>Focus Timer helps scheduling breaks and stepping away. All you need to do is start your first interval.</p>
      </div>
